Solution Explanation  
For a number n to be a power of three it has to satisfy two conditions:

1. n must be positive.  
2. It must divide some power of three exactly.

Inside a 32-bit signed integer the biggest power of three that still fits is  

3¹⁹ = 1 162 261 467 (because 3²⁰ = 3 486 784 401 > 2³¹ – 1).

Therefore every positive power of three is a divisor of 1 162 261 467.  
Conversely, if a positive integer divides 1 162 261 467 without a remainder, it must itself be a power of three.

This observation lets us decide the problem in O(1) time and O(1) memory – no loops, recursion or logarithms are required.

Reference implementation (Java)

```java
public class PowerOfThree {

    // Largest 3^x that fits into a signed 32-bit integer
    private static final int MAX_POWER_OF_THREE = 1_162_261_467; // 3^19

    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // 1. must be positive
        if (n <= 0) return false;

        // 2. only powers of three divide MAX_POWER_OF_THREE exactly
        return (MAX_POWER_OF_THREE % n) == 0;
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------
       Optional: classic iterative version (for completeness)
       ------------------------------------------------------------
       public boolean isPowerOfThreeLoop(int n) {
           if (n <= 0) return false;
           while (n % 3 == 0) {
               n /= 3;
           }
           return n == 1;
       }
    */
}
```

Complexities  
• Time: O(1)  
• Space: O(1)

The above code fulfils the follow-up requirement of solving the task without using loops or recursion.

